# Build context for the root Dockerfile and Dockerfile.embedding_server.
#
# Keep in sync with what those two files actually COPY:
#   Dockerfile                 -> supervisord.conf, run_demos.sh, and the project
#                                  itself: pyproject.toml, uv.lock, README.md,
#                                  LICENSE.md, AUTHORS.txt, bertrend/
#   Dockerfile.embedding_server -> bertrend/services/embedding_server/,
#                                  bertrend/services/{key,cert}.pem
# Everything else can be excluded. Without this file the context is ~8.8G
# (.venv alone is ~8.5G) and is re-sent to the daemon on every build.

# Virtual environments
.venv
venv/
env/
ENV/

# Git
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# Python
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/*$py.class
**/*.so
.Python
**/*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
*.egg

# Caches / test + lint artifacts
.cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Local data, models and runtime state (not needed by any build)
data/
.bertrend/
**/logs/
**/*.log
**/*.sqlite
**/*.db

# Environment files (never needed at build time; may hold credentials)
.env
.env.*
.env_*

# Streamlit secrets. The Dockerfile now COPYs bertrend/ into the build context,
# and /src is not a git checkout -- so the build backend cannot fall back on
# .gitignore to keep these out and would bake real passwords into the image.
# They are bind-mounted at runtime instead (see docker-compose.yml).
**/.streamlit/secrets.toml

# Docs and notebooks
docs/
getting_started/
**/*.md
**/*.ipynb
# ...except these two: pyproject.toml references them as `readme` and `license`,
# so the wheel build fails without them in the context.
!README.md
!LICENSE.md

# Tests
**/tests/
**/test_*.py
**/*_test.py

# IDE / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
.junie/
**/*.swp
**/*.swo
**/*~
**/.DS_Store
**/Thumbs.db

# Docker
Dockerfile*
docker-compose*.yml
.dockerignore
